From b3c6ddf1c91c2a36e20466dd0c51fc9ba2b326b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arttttt <artem-bambalov@yandex.ru>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:31:47 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libartbase: call memfd_create() without asking the kernel
 version

memfd_create() refused to make the call on any kernel older than 3.17 and
returned ENOSYS on its behalf, a guard against old kernels that crash on
the syscall (b/116769556). The mocha kernel is 3.10 with memfd_create and
its seals backported and wired to ARM's syscall 385, so the guard turns a
working call into a missing one.

On S that is fatal. system_server's private JIT region is a dual mapping
of one memfd and, with RWX forbidden to system_server, has no fallback:
JitMemoryRegion::Initialize() fails, PostForkChildAction() only logs
"Could not create private region after zygote fork", the JIT stays on, and
the first compilation dereferences the missing mspace. system_server dies
and zygote restarts with it in a loop.

A kernel without the syscall answers ENOSYS by itself, which is the
fallback the guard existed to fake.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 libartbase/base/memfd.cc | 14 --------------
 1 file changed, 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libartbase/base/memfd.cc b/libartbase/base/memfd.cc
index 8512a3a..526a29e 100644
--- a/libartbase/base/memfd.cc
+++ b/libartbase/base/memfd.cc
@@ -49,20 +49,6 @@ namespace art {
 #if defined(__NR_memfd_create)
 
 int memfd_create(const char* name, unsigned int flags) {
-  // Check kernel version supports memfd_create(). Some older kernels segfault executing
-  // memfd_create() rather than returning ENOSYS (b/116769556).
-  static constexpr int kRequiredMajor = 3;
-  static constexpr int kRequiredMinor = 17;
-  struct utsname uts;
-  int major, minor;
-  if (uname(&uts) != 0 ||
-      strcmp(uts.sysname, "Linux") != 0 ||
-      sscanf(uts.release, "%d.%d", &major, &minor) != 2 ||
-      (major < kRequiredMajor || (major == kRequiredMajor && minor < kRequiredMinor))) {
-    errno = ENOSYS;
-    return -1;
-  }
-
   return syscall(__NR_memfd_create, name, flags);
 }
 
